 {
  "id": "art-0052-nobel-economics-hall-woodford-2026",
  "slug": "hall-woodford-win-2026-nobel-economics-prize",
  "headline": "Robert Hall and Michael Woodford Win 2026 Nobel Prize in Economics",
  "socialHeadline": "Two American economists, Robert Hall and Michael Woodford, have won the 2026 Nobel Prize in economics for rebuilding macroeconomics on microeconomic foundations",
  "summary": "The Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences awarded the 2026 Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences to Robert E. Hall of Stanford and Michael Woodford of Columbia for their work on the microeconomic foundations of macroeconomic stabilisation. The prize carries 12 million Swedish crowns.",
  "body": [
   "The Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences on Monday awarded the 2026 Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences to Robert E. Hall and Michael Woodford, honouring the two American economists for work that rebuilt macroeconomic theory on firmer microeconomic foundations.",
   "Hall, of Stanford University, and Woodford, of Columbia University, were cited \"for microeconomic foundations of macroeconomic stabilisation.\" The prize, worth 12 million Swedish crowns (about $1.2 million), is the last of the 2026 Nobel category prizes to be announced.",
   "## What the laureates did",
   "Modern macroeconomics aims to explain economy-wide phenomena \u2014 recessions, inflation, unemployment \u2014 and to guide the policies that stabilise them. Hall and Woodford's influential research programme reconstructed the models central banks and governments use for stabilisation policy so that they rest on how households and firms actually behave, rather than on ad-hoc aggregates.",
   "Woodford, born in 1955 in Chicopee, Massachusetts, is best known for rigorous treatments of monetary policy under interest-rate rules and for formalising how central-bank communication shapes expectations. Hall, born in 1943 in Palo Alto, California, made foundational contributions to understanding consumption, labour markets and recessions, including influential work on how demand shortfalls propagate through the economy.",
   "## Why it matters",
   "The Academy's citation points to practical payoffs: models with sound microeconomic foundations let policymakers design better-timed fiscal and monetary interventions \u2014 the stabilisation policies deployed during downturns and inflationary episodes.",
   "NewsVeria's take: this year's prize rewards the economics profession's long march toward models in which aggregate outcomes emerge from individual decisions \u2014 the intellectual infrastructure behind modern central banking.",
   "## The ceremony",
   "The laureates will receive their medals and diploma at the Nobel Prize award ceremony in Stockholm in December, as is tradition. The economics prize, formally the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel, was established in 1968."
  ],
  "category": "Business",
  "tags": [
   "Nobel Prize",
   "Economics",
   "Robert Hall",
   "Michael Woodford",
   "Central Banks"
  ],
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T11:19:57Z",
  "image": {
   "src": "/images/media-generation-nv-nobel-economics-20261012.webp",
