 {
  "id": "art-0053-norway-fines-xandr-2026",
  "slug": "norway-fines-microsoft-xandr-96-million-grindr-data-sharing",
  "headline": "Norway Fines Microsoft's Xandr $9.6 Million Over Grindr Data Sharing",
  "socialHeadline": "Norway's data watchdog fined Microsoft's ad-tech unit Xandr a record $9.6 million for piping Grindr users' sensitive data to ad auctions",
  "summary": "Norway's Data Protection Authority fined Xandr, Microsoft's advertising-technology unit, 92 million kroner (about $9.6 million) \u2014 its largest fine ever \u2014 for unlawfully sharing Grindr users' sensitive personal data with advertisers through real-time bidding between 2018 and 2020.",
  "body": [
   "Norway's Data Protection Authority, Datatilsynet, on Monday imposed a 92-million-kroner fine (about $9.6 million) on Xandr, the advertising-technology business owned by Microsoft, over the unlawful sharing of Grindr users' sensitive personal data. It is the largest fine the regulator has ever issued.",
   "The authority found that Xandr had relayed data revealing users' sexual orientation \u2014 considered sensitive personal data under European privacy law \u2014 to potentially thousands of advertisers through real-time-bidding ad auctions between July 2018 and April 2020, without a valid legal basis.",
   "## Consent argument rejected",
   "Xandr had argued it could rely on assurances from Grindr that users had consented to the data sharing. Datatilsynet rejected that defence, finding that the consent relied upon was not valid for the onward sharing that took place in the auction system.",
   "The case began with a 2020 complaint by the Norwegian Consumer Council together with the privacy organisation noyb. The fine was calculated on the basis of Xandr's revenue; notably, Microsoft did not own Xandr during the period of the violations \u2014 it acquired the company later.",
   "## What happens next",
   "Xandr has three weeks to appeal the decision. Microsoft said it was reviewing the decision.",
   "The ruling is the latest sign of European regulators tightening enforcement against the opaque data flows of programmatic advertising, where personal data can be broadcast to hundreds of bidders in the milliseconds before an ad loads.",
   "## The wider pattern",
   "Real-time bidding systems have repeatedly drawn scrutiny from privacy regulators across Europe, who argue that broadcasting personal data to vast numbers of potential advertisers cannot satisfy the GDPR's requirements for consent, transparency and data minimisation. Norway's record fine puts that enforcement stance into sharper focus."
  ],
  "category": "Technology",
  "tags": [
   "Norway",
   "Microsoft",
   "Xandr",
   "Grindr",
   "Data Privacy",
   "GDPR"
  ],
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
