 {
  "id": "art-0054-riyadh-airport-suspension-2026",
  "slug": "riyadh-airport-extends-suspension-after-houthi-attacks",
  "headline": "Riyadh Airport Extends Suspension After Weekend Houthi Attacks",
  "socialHeadline": "Saudi Arabia extended the shutdown of Riyadh's main airport after weekend Houthi strikes killed 13 and wounded hundreds",
  "summary": "Saudi Arabia's civil aviation authority extended the suspension of operations at Riyadh's King Khalid International Airport on Monday, giving no resumption date, after weekend Houthi attacks killed 13 people and wounded more than 300 in the deadliest strikes on Saudi soil in over a decade.",
  "body": [
   "Saudi Arabia's General Authority of Civil Aviation announced on Monday that it was extending the suspension of operations at King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh, with no resumption date specified, \"to complete the necessary procedures to verify the safety of the facilities and their operational readiness.\"",
   "The decision follows a weekend of Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia. A Saturday strike killed 13 people and wounded more than 300 \u2014 described as the deadliest attack on Saudi soil in over a decade and claimed by the Houthis \u2014 while a second attack on Sunday killed one person and injured about 11.",
   "## Flights cancelled, airlines pull out",
   "Aviation data provider Cirium reported that the airport cancelled 99.7 percent of scheduled flights on Sunday; FlightRadar24 estimated about 78 percent of Monday's flights cancelled.",
   "The fallout spread across the industry: Lufthansa Group cancelled all Riyadh flights until October 16, Air France-KLM extended its suspension to the end of October, and Etihad further extended its Saudi cancellations. The US Federal Aviation Administration told American carriers on Sunday to exercise caution when flying to or over Saudi Arabia.",
   "## A widening conflict",
   "The strikes are part of an escalation in which Yemen's Iran-backed Houthis have stepped up attacks on Saudi airports and energy infrastructure as well as on tankers in the Red Sea. King Khalid International is Saudi Arabia's second-busiest airport after Jeddah's.",
   "NewsVeria will update this story as the airport authority announces a reopening timeline."
  ],
